Constraints may be nonlinear in the labels (\code{cp == a*b}, \code{a*b == 0.1},
\code{a*b > 0.1}, \code{ab > 0} with \code{ab := a*b}), solved with the Jacobian recomputed at
every point; a model with one tries every start and keeps the best accepted
solution, warning when the starts disagree. For such a model the residual
variances that appear in a nonlinear row get a lower bound of \code{1e-4} times their
natural unit (the variable's variance), because a step to a negative variance is
outside the row's domain; the fit warns when a bound is active, and a residual
variance below that bound (an R-squared above 0.9999) is not fitted exactly. The
model without the nonlinear rows is fitted first: its estimate, and copies of it
with the signs of the regression paths in the rows flipped (a product constraint
cannot change sign along its surface, so each sign pattern is its own branch),
are extra starts, and a feasible reference estimate is never replaced by a worse
accepted fit. Models without a nonlinear row are not affected. A product of labels set to zero
(\code{a*b == 0}, \code{b1*b2*b3 == 0}) is instead solved exactly: one linear fit per
factor (\code{a == 0}, \code{b == 0}), keeping the smallest discrepancy; if a branch cannot be fitted, \code{fit_sem()}
stops with a \code{medfit_error_no_convergence} error instead of returning the best of the others; the error carries
the outcome ledger of every branch as \verb{$ledger} (see \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}}). A sum of products
(\code{a1*b1 + a2*b2 == 0}) has no exact reduction, so it is best-effort: the fit is
the best of the starts tried, a disagreement warning means a better optimum may
exist, and \code{n_starts} raises the number of starts. Constrained standard errors use the active tangent space:
observed information includes nonlinear constraint
curvature, while expected information retains its Fisher definition. Active inequalities and bounds, including
solver variance floors, give a local tangent approximation rather than the full boundary sampling distribution.
See \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}} and the Methods and Formulas article for details. Labels used in a constraint
or a definition must be defined in the model. A constraint with no free
parameter is an error. Exogenous variables are given free
variances and covariances, as lavaan does with \code{fixed.x = FALSE}. An exogenous variable named in any \verb{~~}
statement is left out of that pairing: its variance and the covariances the model writes are kept, and its
covariances with the other exogenous variables are 0 unless written (lavaan does the same). \code{fixed_x = TRUE}
(or a vector of names) in \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}} fixes the moments of the selected ones at the
sample values instead, as \code{fixed.x = TRUE} does. Unlike lavaan, which keeps an
explicit \code{x ~~ x} or \code{x ~ 1} free under \code{fixed.x = TRUE} and lets the degrees of
freedom move, medfit stops with an error when the model has a statement on a
selected variable's moments. Any
intercept statement (\code{y ~ 1}, or \code{1} as a term: \code{y ~ 1 + x}) turns the mean structure
on and frees every observed intercept the model does not write, as lavaan does;
\code{y ~ 0*1} then restricts the mean of \code{y} to 0 (one degree of freedom), and
\code{meanstructure = TRUE} in \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}} turns it on without any statement, and a product term
(\code{x:m}, see Product terms) always turns it on. A
latent variable's mean is fixed at 0; a free latent \code{~1} is an error. A repeated \code{1} for one
variable is an error.
A variable cannot appear on both sides of \code{~} or \verb{=~} (\code{y ~ y}), and a path,
a (co)variance or an intercept takes one row: a repeated \code{y ~ x} is an error,
not a second row that competes for the label.
