\section{Factor covariates}{

A factor, character or logical column named on a regression's right side is replaced by
one 0/1 dummy per non-reference level, named \verb{<column>_<level>} (\code{g} with levels \code{a}, \code{b},
\code{c} gives \code{g_b} and \code{g_c}). The reference level is the first factor level; a character
column sorts in the C locale, so the result does not depend on the session locale. A
logical column gives one dummy, \verb{<column>_TRUE}. Dummies are built from the full column
before rows with missing values are dropped, and a missing value in the factor drops the
row once. \code{tidy()} and the parameter table show the dummies by name, and the fit's data
holds the dummies, not the factor.\tabular{ll}{
   Limit \tab Message (starts with, or contains) \cr
   An ordered factor \tab "is an ordered factor" \cr
   A factor with one level \tab "has only one level" \cr
   A level that makes an invalid name \tab "makes the invalid name" \cr
   A dummy that is already a column \tab "is already a column of \code{data}" \cr
   A label, fixed value, start or bound on a factor (\code{b*g}) \tab "expands to" \cr
   A constraint that names the factor (\code{g == 0}) \tab "is a categorical column" \cr
   A product of two factors \tab "both sides are categorical columns" \cr
   A dummy with no variation in the rows used \tab "is constant in the rows the fit uses" \cr
   A factor as the outcome or a mediator \tab "must be numeric" \cr
   A factor as treatment or mediator in \code{extract_mediation()} \tab "is a factor in the model" \cr
}


A label goes on one dummy: \code{b*g_b} works, \code{b*g} stops, and a constraint names the label
(\code{b == 0}), never the factor. A bootstrap that refits from raw data re-expands the factor in each
resample, so a level missing from a character column's resample changes the reference level.
\code{g:m} expands to \code{g_b:m} and \code{g_c:m}, each a product term under the rules below. \code{fixed_x = "g"} fixes every dummy of
\code{g}, and a dummy name fixes one. A factor covariate in the mediator equation enters the
four-way covariate means through its dummies; a factor treatment or mediator is not
supported (recode a two-level factor as a 0/1 numeric column), and the four-way route
refuses a \code{g:m} product because its effects differ by level.
}

\section{Product terms}{

\code{x:m} is the product of two observed variables, written with no spaces, on the
right side of a regression (\code{y ~ x + m + x:m}). As in lavaan, the product is an
observed exogenous variable: the engine builds the column \code{x * m} from \code{data}
after listwise deletion, with a free variance, free covariances with the other
exogenous variables and (the mean structure is on) a free mean. The likelihood
does not change. \code{x:m} and \code{m:x} are one term, stored and printed under the
canonical name, the two names sorted in the C locale (\code{m:x}); the coefficient is
named \code{y~m:x}, or by its label (\code{b*x:m} labels the whole product).
\code{extract_mediation()} finds the treatment-by-mediator product through the same
name and returns the four-way decomposition; centering the mediator moves the
split between the controlled direct effect and the reference interaction, not
the natural effects.

A column in \code{data} named \code{m:x} or \code{x:m} is used only if it equals \code{x * m} on the
rows kept; otherwise the fit stops. A product term turns the mean structure on,
and \code{meanstructure = FALSE} is then an error. A coefficient equals the
equation-wise least-squares estimate inside the class below, and the sandwich
covariance (\code{se_type = "sandwich"}) is the standard-error choice, because a
product is not normal; the engine warns once when it is not used. \code{fixed_x}
includes the product columns.\tabular{ll}{
   Limit \tab Message (starts with, or contains) \cr
   A space around \code{:} \tab "a product ':' needs a variable name on each side, written without spaces (x:m)" \cr
   \code{x:x} \tab "of a variable with itself is not supported" \cr
   \code{x:m:w} \tab "a three-way or higher product" \cr
   Product as a left side (\code{x:m ~ y}, \code{x:m ~~ y}) \tab "cannot be a left side" \cr
   Product in \verb{=~} \tab "is only valid on the right side of a regression" \cr
   Product inside a constraint or \verb{:=} \tab "not inside the expression" \cr
   A latent variable in a product \tab "products with latent variables are not supported in this version" \cr
   \code{y ~ x:m + m:x} (one product twice in an equation) \tab "are the same product (canonical name" \cr
   A factor that is the target or downstream of it (\code{y ~ x + x:y}) \tab "(rule 8: no outcome-derived regressors)" \cr
   A loop of regressions \tab "The model is not recursive" \cr
   A residual covariance between outcomes (\code{m1 ~~ m2}) \tab "cannot have a residual covariance (rule 9)" \cr
   A label shared by two rows \tab "is used on more than one parameter" \cr
   A fixed coefficient (\code{0.3*x}, \code{0*x}) \tab "has a fixed coefficient (rule 9)" \cr
   A bound (\code{lower(0)*x}) \tab "has a bound (rule 9" \cr
   \code{==}, \code{<} or \code{>} naming a coefficient label \tab "names the coefficient label" \cr
   A fixed, shared or constrained intercept \tab "(rule 9, intercept clause" \cr
   \code{meanstructure = FALSE} with a product \tab "\code{meanstructure = FALSE}, but the model has a product term" \cr
   A column \code{m:x} in \code{data} that is not \code{x * m} \tab "is not the product of" \cr
   A product input that is not numeric, factor, character or logical (a date) \tab "needs numeric variables" \cr
   \code{information = "expected"}, endogenous factor \tab "with a product of an endogenous variable" \cr
   \code{anova()} or \code{mbco_sem()} on such a fit \tab "is not available for ... a model with a product term" \cr
   Another product on the treatment or mediator, in \code{extract_mediation()} \tab "does not support the product" \cr
}


Rules 8 and 9 are checked on the parsed table, so they need no data. Rule 8
rejects a regressor built from the outcome (it is correlated with the equation's
error); rule 9 names what is not yet supported with products, and the same
model works without the product. The covariance that completion would add
between two pure outcomes is fixed at 0 in a product model, with one note.
Products of observed variables only: a product with a latent variable, \code{x:x} and
three-way products are not supported.

The global chi-square is not valid when a factor of a product is itself a
regression target (the model treats the product as exogenous although it is a
function of that variable) or when the exogenous (co)variance block is
restricted. \code{glance()} then reports chi-square, df and p-value as \code{NA} with a
note, \code{anova()} and \code{\link[=mbco_sem]{mbco_sem()}} refuse, and expected information is refused for
an endogenous product; \code{logLik()}, AIC and BIC stay defined, but those of a fit with a
product are not comparable with those of a fit without one, so \code{AIC(fit_a, fit_b)}, \code{BIC()}, \code{anova()} and
\code{\link[=mbco_sem]{mbco_sem()}} refuse such a pair. A product of
exogenous variables with the block free keeps the ordinary test.
}

\section{Defaults changed in 0.15.0}{

Since medfit 0.15.0 an unset \code{fixed_x} means the exogenous observed variables (as lavaan's \code{fixed.x = TRUE}) and an
unset \code{meanstructure} means \code{TRUE}, so every observed intercept is free without a \code{~1} statement. Against 0.14.x the
parameter count, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()} and the parameter names move; the structural estimates and their
standard errors did not move on the cases measured. A variable a \verb{~~} or \code{~1} statement names is left free (message
class \code{medfit_message_fixed_x_lenient}) instead of the error an explicit \code{fixed_x = TRUE} gives. To keep the 0.14.x
results, pass \verb{fixed_x = FALSE, meanstructure = NULL}. The 0.14.x \code{options(medfit.native_defaults)} opt-in no longer
has any effect. \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}} has the details.
}

\section{medfit extensions}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Comma shorthand} (medfit extension). A comma-separated left side
expands to one statement per name: \verb{y1, y2 ~ x} means \code{y1 ~ x} and
\code{y2 ~ x}. Commas on the right side are not supported; use \code{+}.
\strong{lavaan reads \verb{y1, y2 ~ x} differently: it silently keeps only \code{y2}.}
A model copied between the two packages can therefore change meaning
without an error.
\item \strong{\code{CONSTRAINT(...)}} (medfit extension). A constraint line may be
written \code{CONSTRAINT(a == b)}, equivalent to the bare \code{a == b}. The keyword
is case-sensitive and starts the statement, one pair of parentheses
encloses the whole constraint, it holds exactly one comparison, and no
text follows the closing parenthesis.
}
}

\section{Expressions}{

Both sides of a constraint and the right side of a definition are
arithmetic expressions in the labels, with \verb{+ - * / ^}, parentheses, finite
numbers, and the functions \code{exp}, \code{log}, \code{log10}, \code{sqrt}, \code{abs}, \code{min}, \code{max},
\code{sin}, \code{cos}, \code{tan}, \code{pnorm} and \code{qnorm}. Anything else is
rejected with the construct named.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
# Labeled paths and a defined parameter
fit_sem(
  "mediator1 ~ a*treatment\noutcome ~ b*mediator1 + cp*treatment\nab := a*b",
  data = mediation_demo, fixed_x = FALSE, meanstructure = NULL
)@defined

# Comma shorthand: one left-hand side per name
coef(fit_sem(
  "mediator1, mediator2 ~ treatment\noutcome ~ mediator1 + mediator2 + treatment",
  data = mediation_demo
))[1:3]

# An equality constraint, written with the CONSTRAINT() keyword
coef(fit_sem(
  "mediator1 ~ a*treatment\noutcome ~ b*mediator1 + treatment\nCONSTRAINT(a == b)",
  data = mediation_demo
))[c("a", "b")]
}

}
